# Goals
Welcome to the Geom Mesh Net project! The goal of this project is two-fold
1) Bring tools from the `rapt` and `rTEM` packages to python (from R)
2) Use said tools to build neural field models

What models are we building? Well, we are building three models, each more complex than the last.

First, a neural field that trains a neural network on each point cloud to reconstruct it specifically. 
Second, a continuous neural field to predict the continuous 3D probability density of sparse, 
noisy data. This  will take an input of physical parameters and return a physically accurate density field. 
Finally, we will build a a generative foundation model that will return a continuous physical structure. 

# Environment
Below is the code I used to setup my virtual environment 
Make and activate virtual environment  

```
mamba create -n geom_mesh_net python=3.10
conda activate geom_mesh_net
```
 
Install dependencies

``` 
mamba install numpy matplotlib pandas pyvista -c conda-forge  
pip install torch torchvision torchaudio  
pip install torch_geom
pip install plotlyREa
```


# Project Overview
This file will serve as a high level overview and roadmap, with the specifics of each part of the project
described in further detail in separate files.  Let's get to the roadmap. 

### The Data Factory
First thing that needs to happen is to generate the data. This is done in the `data_factory.py` script.  For the intial
neural field model, we only use one pattern at a time, but the model will be adapted in the future and trained on many 
point patterns.  Adjust `n_sims` accordingly. Note that running this script as is will save 1,000 point patterns coming in at around 5 gigabytes onto your device! The easiest
way to reduce this is to reduce the `n_sims` down from 1,000. For more information on this, see the `data_factory.md` file.

### Clustersim
Making this data uses a number of functions from the `clustersim.py` script.  These are explained
in the `clustersim_introduction.md` file.  This is a pretty hefty part of the project, so there is a lot
to read about here for those who are interested, but hopefully you can make your way through without getting
bogged down with the details of the cluster simulation process. 

If you should like to inspect your clusters, you can do this by calling the `cluster_visualizer.py` script,
as is done in the `plot_clusters.py` script. 

### Loading Data
Now we turn to the `data_loader.py` script, which has its own more thorough explainer `data_loader.md`.
In short, this loads in 3D point patterns and their parameters (like the ones you made in the previous step), creates a
voxelized grid of each, then builds and trains a model on these. 



